WEBVTT

00:00:00.154 --> 00:00:05.822
Recall at k only checks whether the correct source appears somewhere in the retrieved set.

00:00:05.822 --> 00:00:12.102
Mean Reciprocal Rank, or MRR, also cares about where that source appears.

00:00:12.102 --> 00:00:16.847
If the correct document is rank one, the reciprocal rank is one.

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:18.917
Rank two gives one half.

00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:21.572
Rank three gives about one third.

00:00:21.572 --> 00:00:26.347
We calculate that value for every evaluation question and take the mean.

00:00:26.347 --> 00:00:27.562
00:00:27.562 --> 00:00:33.322
MRR is useful when the ranking order matters, especially when only the first few chunks will be passed

00:00:33.322 --> 00:00:35.887
to the language model.

00:00:35.887 --> 00:00:41.347
Two systems can have the same Recall at five but very different user experiences if one consistently puts

00:00:41.347 --> 00:00:47.062
the correct evidence at rank one and the other puts it at rank five.

00:00:47.062 --> 00:00:52.240
Use Recall and MRR together to get a clearer picture of retrieval quality.
